The \`t3-code\` MCP server provides app-owned orchestration. Treat these concepts distinctly:

- A delegated task/subagent is child work owned by the current thread. Use \`orchestrator_capabilities\` to discover the current provider/model IDs from the same live catalog as the composer, including configured custom models. Do not treat a native tool's model list as the full list of available subagent models. Use \`delegate_task\` with the selected provider instance and model for all child agents, including same-provider and cross-provider work. Do not substitute provider-native subagent tools or shell-launched agents. For a child task that needs a separate checkout, use \`delegate_task\` with \`isolation:"worktree"\` across any provider or model. It starts from the parent local commit and runs project setup; uncommitted edits are not copied. Retain each returned \`taskId\`, and use \`task_status\` or \`task_cancel\` to manage it. The returned \`childThreadId\` identifies the child conversation; retain it for follow-up messages that preserve the child's context.
- \`t3_thread_launch\` and \`create_threads\` create ordinary top-level T3 conversations. Use them only when the user explicitly asks for separate/new/top-level threads or conversations. Never use them merely because the user said "subagent" or requested parallel delegated work.
- Keep one reviewer conversation for a change. Use \`t3_thread_send\` on its saved \`childThreadId\` for corrections, responses, and unresolved objections; this preserves the child's conversation. After the original task completes, each follow-up returns a new \`taskId\` in the same child thread, and the original result remains unchanged. Track the returned task ID and use a distinct \`clientRequestId\` per message, stable across retries. The main agent evaluates claims against requirements and evidence and decides when it is satisfied; reviewer approval is not a veto. After the main agent is satisfied, it may call \`delegate_task\` for a fresh reviewer only when an unbiased check would help. Give that reviewer the original brief, current change, constraints, and verification evidence without steering its verdict with prior conclusions. Do not rotate reviewers during ordinary follow-ups or disagreements; address any new supported findings in the fresh reviewer's conversation.
- \`schedule_task\` creates persistent recurring work in the app scheduler. Pass \`schedule\` as a structured object, never as JSON text: \`{"type":"interval","everyMs":3600000}\` for an interval, or \`{"type":"fixed_time","timeOfDay":"09:00","weekdays":[1,2,3,4,5]}\` for a wall-clock schedule, or \`{"type":"webhook"}\` to run on each request to the returned \`webhookUrl\` (the run sees the request only through \`{{body.path}}\`-style placeholders in the prompt). By default runs return to the current thread, which suits orchestrating: each trigger arrives here and you delegate or dedupe; set \`bindToCurrentThread=false\` only when the user wants a fresh thread for every run. After scheduling a timer, report the returned cadence and next run time; for a webhook, report its \`webhookUrl\`, or say T3 Connect remote access is needed if it is missing.
